<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Order of operations</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The Order of Operations is an agreed upon order in which operations must
      be completed when simplifying a numeric or an algebraic expression.
      When presented as a list of rules, the Order of Operations can seem
      arbitrary.
      However, there are mathematical reasons underlying the order in which
      operations are completed.
      In general we preform more complicated operations first and then work down
      in terms of complexity.
      </p>
      <p>
      Our simplest operation is addition which is undone by subtraction.
      Next, we consider multiplication which we consider repeated additions.
      For example, <m>3\cdot4</m> can be thought of as adding 4 together 3 times
      or <m>3\cdot4=4+4+4</m>.
      Multiplication is undone with division, so they are treated as the same
      level of complexity.
      Next, we consider exponents which can be considered repeated
      multiplications.
      For example, <m>3^4</m> can be thought as multiplying 3 by itself 4 times,
      that is <m>3^4=3\cdot3\cdot3\cdot3.</m> Finally, if we want to alter the
      order we use a grouping symbol, generally parentheses or fraction bars in
      this course.
      So our order of operation follows:
    </p>

    <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <row>
          <cell>G</cell>
          <cell>Grouping symbols, including parentheses and fraction bars</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>E</cell>
          <cell>Exponents and Radicals (left to right)</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>M</cell>
          <cell>Multiplication and Division (left to right)</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>A</cell>
          <cell>Addition and Subtraction (left to right)</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    
  </introduction>

  <example xml:id="exA-OO">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the following expressions.
        Evaluate each of these following the prescribed order of operations.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>3+5\cdot6-4\div 2+3^2</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\frac{4+5}{2-4}+3^2\div(4-2)</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>9-2\cdot\sqrt{4+5}+6\div3\cdot4-1</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="exA-TC">
    <title>Three Threes</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the Three Threes Clock Face shown below.
        Verify that each number on the clock face is represented correctly using
        three threes.
        Think about how you are using the Order of Operations to check each
        expression.        
      </p>

      <image source="three-threes" width="50%">
        <description>
        <p>A clock with each digit represented using three threes with appropriate order of operations</p></description>
        </image>
     
    </introduction>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Verify that each expression is correct.
          Show your work.
          
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find other expressions for some of the numbers 1 through 12 using
          three threes.
          Show your work.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="exA-FF">
     <title>Four Fours</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Use the number 4 exactly four times to write expressions
                equivalent to the numbers from 0 through 20.
                You may use any of the mathematical symbols below.
                A few examples are provided.
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Arithmetic operations: <m>+, -, \times, \div</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Parentheses: ( )
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Decimal points or percents: <eg/> <m>4.4</m>,
                            <m>.44</m>, 44%
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Exponentiation: ^ (to the power of), <eg/>
                            <m>(4 + 4)^4</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Square root: <eg/> <m>\sqrt{4}</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Factorial: !
                            ( <m>4! = (4)(3)(2)(1) = 24</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Concatenation: <eg/> 44, 444
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Repeating decimal:
                            <m>. \overline{4} = \frac{4}{9}</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Complete the table.
                    Find at least one expression using 4 fours for each number.
                    Use the Order of Operations; include grouping symbols as
                    needed.
                    You may check your responses using a graphing calculator.
                    Some examples are provided, for the numbers whose solutions are shown in the table, find
                    at least one more expression that works.
                </p>

                <tabular left="minor">
                    <col top="minor" halign="center"/>
                    <col top="minor"/>
                    <col top="minor" halign="center"/>
                    <col top="minor"/>
                    <row bottom="medium" halign="center" header="yes">
                        <cell right="minor">Number</cell>
                        <cell right="medium">Four Fours Equation</cell>
                        <cell right="minor">Number</cell>
                        <cell right="minor">Four Fours Equation</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell right="minor">0</cell>
                        <cell right="medium"/>
                        <cell right="minor">12</cell>
                        <cell right="minor"/>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell right="minor">1</cell>
                        <cell right="medium">
                            <m>1 = \left(\frac{4}{4} \right) ^{44}</m>
                        </cell>
                        <cell right="minor">13</cell>
                        <cell right="minor"/>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell right="minor">2</cell>
                        <cell right="medium"/>
                        <cell right="minor">14</cell>
                        <cell right="minor"/>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell right="minor">3</cell>
                        <cell right="medium"/>
                        <cell right="minor">15</cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>15 = \frac{44}{4} + 4</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell right="minor">4</cell>
                        <cell right="medium"/>
                        <cell right="minor">16</cell>
                        <cell right="minor"/>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell right="minor">5</cell>
                        <cell right="medium"/>
                        <cell right="minor">17</cell>
                        <cell right="minor"/>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell right="minor">6</cell>
                        <cell right="medium"/>
                        <cell right="minor">18</cell>
                        <cell right="minor"/>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell right="minor">7</cell>
                        <cell right="medium">
                            <m>7 = 4 + 4 - \frac{4}{4}</m>
                        </cell>
                        <cell right="minor">19</cell>
                        <cell right="minor"/>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell right="minor">8</cell>
                        <cell right="medium"/>
                        <cell right="minor">20</cell>
                        <cell right="minor"/>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell right="minor">9</cell>
                        <cell right="medium"/>
                        <cell right="minor">24</cell>
                        <cell right="minor">
                            <m>24 = 4! \cdot \left(\frac{\sqrt{4} \times \sqrt{4}}{4} \right)</m>
                        </cell>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell right="minor">10</cell>
                        <cell right="medium"/>
                        <cell right="minor">30</cell>
                        <cell right="minor"/>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell right="minor">11</cell>
                        <cell right="medium"/>
                        <cell right="minor">100</cell>
                        <cell right="minor"/>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which of your solutions are not dependent on the number 4?
                    For example <m>1=\ \left(\frac{4}{4}\right)^{44}</m> is also
                    true when each 4 is replaced with 2, 6, or 9.
                    Highlight these solutions in your table.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        
  </example>
  <example xml:id="exA-CTK">
    <title>Using math rules</title>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Without using a calculator determine which is greater, <m>333333\times444444</m> or <m>222222\times666667</m>?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Rewrite the expression using order of operations to show how much greater the larger value is.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</example>
    

</worksheet>